  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA few showers will be possible in the mountains this afternoon \nbut a dry day is expected for most. Due to a relatively warm \nstart early this morning and strong downslope flow during the \nday, temperatures should rise into the 80s at most lower \nelevations, with 70s in the mountains. The main story will be \nthe gusty westerly winds. Great low level mixing is expected \nwhich should allow 25 to 35 MPH gusts most locations. BUFKIT \nforecast soundings show the potential for a few 40-45 MPH gusts \nat times. There could be a few power outages with fully leaved \ntrees. Higher gusts expected in the mountains. Expect wind gusts\nover 65 MPH at times above 4500 ft.\n\nCold air advection will continue tonight albeit with diminishing\nwinds. Lows are expected to dip into the lower 40s in the\nmountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nFor Friday, expect cooler, dry, and breezy conditions with northwest \nwinds gusting up to 25 mph through the first part of the day. Highs \nwill mainly range from the low to mid 60s north, to the low to mid \n70s south. Winds then relax under a clear sky Friday night which \nwill lead to favorable radiational cooling conditions. Expect \nwidespread lows in the low to mid 40s with some mid to upper 30s \npossible in the northern valleys.\n\nThe next trough and front will approach on Sunday, leading to our \nnext chances of low impact precipitation. Additional low chances of \nrain will be possible through mid next week. Expect highs mainly in \nthe 70s through the weekend and into the next work week with a \ncouple of lower 80s across far southern New Hampshire.",
